      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in asphalt overlays in Phoenix could primarily be caused by the asphalt layer's inability to accommodate the region's extreme diurnal temperature fluctuations. These fluctuations lead to significant thermal expansion and contraction, straining the pavement. Bituminous materials in the overlay may have inadequate bitumen content or poor viscosity at low temperatures, contributing to a reduced capacity to resist the tensile stress and leading to cracking. Proper compaction, use of high performance asphalt mix, and incorporating techniques like reflective cracking or adding fiber reinforcement can help mitigate these issues.",
